WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.840
From movie monster to horror icon, M3GAN could&nbsp;
be the monstrous voice of a new generation.

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:11.640
New horror villains drop all the time, and&nbsp;
compelling characters like "Malignant's"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:16.560
Gabriel and "The Babadook's" titular entity&nbsp;
show that filmmakers have no shortage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:20.400
imagination when it comes to creating&nbsp;
visually impressive villains. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.200
apart from the Babadook's unlikely&nbsp;
stint as a meme-worthy LGBTQ+ icon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:30.780
most horror monsters have a long way to go&nbsp;
before they really reach the status of an "icon."

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:37.920
"Babadook dook DOOK"

00:00:37.920 --> 00:00:42.480
"M3GAN," on the other hand, was a different&nbsp;
beast from the point that the first trailer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.860
dropped. When the film itself turned out to be&nbsp;
even more entertaining than the doll's dance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:52.500
moves in the promotional material, it was clear&nbsp;
that Gen Z has received its first horror icon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:59.460
Megan the killer friend-bot is sassy, smart,&nbsp;
funny, infinitely meme-able, and ... relatable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:05.760
somehow. What's more, her potential to become&nbsp;
a genre mainstay is vast, and everyone involved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:10.920
knows it. Universal was already interested in&nbsp;
a "M3GAN" sequel before the movie premiered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:17.460
and after a successful $30 million debut,&nbsp;
the age of "M3GAN" has officially just begun.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:18.950
"You should probably run."

00:01:18.950 --> 00:01:23.580
While murder clowns and axe killers are&nbsp;
just as terrifying to younger generations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:27.720
as they are to any other cohort of&nbsp;
people, Gen Z is notably the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:32.460
truly digital generation that has never&nbsp;
known a world without the internet. Thus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.460 --> 00:01:38.700
your average Gen Zer is likely far more personally&nbsp;
familiar with advanced technology than they are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:43.260
with circuses or wood-cutting implements.&nbsp;
Right away, that's a point for "M3GAN,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:48.420
whose horror and humor are technology-based.&nbsp;
There's also the fact that the creepy doll's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:53.460
very nature as a go-getter who values its&nbsp;
loved ones and has an extremely practical,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.460 --> 00:01:58.680
though murderous, approach to problem-solving&nbsp;
is quite close to Gen Z's core values.

00:01:58.680 --> 00:02:02.760
Roberta Katz of Stanford University&nbsp;
describes Gen Z thoughtfully,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:08.040
"...a typical Gen Zer is a self-driver who&nbsp;
deeply cares about others, strives for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:13.020
diverse community, is highly collaborative&nbsp;
and social, values flexibility, relevance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:18.780
authenticity and non-hierarchical leadership,&nbsp;
and, while dismayed about inherited issues like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.640
climate change, has a pragmatic attitude about the&nbsp;
work that has to be done to address those issues."

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:30.000
Of course, as Variety notes, a clever, shrewd&nbsp;
marketing strategy that went hard at Tiktok&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:33.960
and Twitter memeability played a part in&nbsp;
giving "M3GAN" its deserved horror queen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:39.780
crown. Going viral has translated into&nbsp;
both cultural status and box-office love.

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:44.700
It's almost certain that a torrent of imitators&nbsp;
will attempt to recreate "M3GAN's" success with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:49.740
their own takes on memeable, marketable horror&nbsp;
that guns for viral attention in hopes of creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.360
horror icons of their own. Unfortunately, it's&nbsp;
also pretty likely that things won't be that easy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.360 --> 00:03:01.320
A horror icon like the M3gan robot is lightning in&nbsp;
a bottle — rare and tough to replicate, even when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:06.780
all the ingredients are clearly visible. Besides,&nbsp;
since "M3GAN" will likely occupy the collective "&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:12.180
new horror icon" headspace for the immediate&nbsp;
future, there's not much room for newcomers.

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:17.280
As such, it may be more interesting to&nbsp;
view "M3GAN" as the spearhead of a general&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.840
horror trend that's been emerging in recent&nbsp;
years and only seems to be growing stronger.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:27.120
Horror has always been at its strongest&nbsp;
when it draws from familiar experience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:32.280
and recent movies have been heavily inspired&nbsp;
by experiences that are far more inherently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.860
familiar to Gen Z than immortal machete men&nbsp;
stalking summer camps can ever hope to be.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:43.980
From "Candyman" to "Get Out," horror has always&nbsp;
been excellent at tackling social and cultural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:49.200
affairs. Now that more and more Gen Z filmmakers&nbsp;
are hitting their horror stride, the focus has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:54.240
simply shifted to what they know well, shedding&nbsp;
the pre-online world of their predecessors in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:04:01.166
process. This will no doubt result in countless&nbsp;
amazing horror films and icons in the future.

